179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 116 0 R
/T (Loan approval is one of the most consequential decisions in retail banking. Approving a borrower who later defaults creates credit losses, while rejecting a creditworthy applicant means lost business. This project, CreditWise, presents a machine-learning approach for predicting whether a loan application will be approved, using a dataset of 1,000 applicant records with 20 original columns and an approval rate of approximately 29.8%. )
/E (Loan approval is one of the most consequential decisions in retail banking. Approving a borrower who later defaults creates credit losses, while rejecting a creditworthy applicant means lost business. This project, CreditWise, presents a machine-learning approach for predicting whether a loan application will be approved, using a dataset of 1,000 applicant records with 20 original columns and an approval rate of approximately 29.8%. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 116 0 R
/T (The workflow includes missing-value handling, removal of the applicant identifier, categorical encoding, exploratory analysis, and binary classification. Numerical columns are filled with the mean and categorical columns with the most frequent value; Education_Level and the target are label encoded and six further categorical columns are one-hot encoded, giving 27 input features. An 80/20 train-test split is used, and standard scaling is applied for the linear, distance-based and probabilistic models. Eight classifiers are compared: Logistic Regression, KNN, Naive Bayes, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, AdaBoost and a Stacking Classifier, with the tree-ensemble models tuned using GridSearchCV with 5-fold cross-validation. )
/E (The workflow includes missing-value handling, removal of the applicant identifier, categorical encoding, exploratory analysis, and binary classification. Numerical columns are filled with the mean and categorical columns with the most frequent value; Education_Level and the target are label encoded and six further categorical columns are one-hot encoded, giving 27 input features. An 80/20 train-test split is used, and standard scaling is applied for the linear, distance-based and probabilistic models. Eight classifiers are compared: Logistic Regression, KNN, Naive Bayes, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, AdaBoost and a Stacking Classifier, with the tree-ensemble models tuned using GridSearchCV with 5-fold cross-validation. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
